      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate fragment carefully.\n\n**Certificate level:**\n- Number: \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04 Rev. 3\" — this is the certificate number. The number itself is R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04, revision 3. I should probably put the full thing in number, or split. The schema has `revision_history[]` for revisions. The number field should probably be \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04\" with a revision history entry for Rev. 3. But we don't know the date of Rev 3. Hmm. The certificate shows \"Rev. 3\" and says \"The present revision of this certificate is the only valid and replaces all previous revisions.\" I'll put number as \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04\" and add revision_history entry {revision: \"3\"} — but date and changes are unknown, so omit them per rule 8 (don't fabricate). Actually the schema says revision_history entries have revision, date, changes. I could include {revision: \"3\"} with only revision. That seems reasonable. Alternatively include number as full string \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04 Rev. 3\". I think cleaner: number: \"R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04\", and revision_history: [{revision: \"3\"}]. Hmm, but revision_history entry with missing date/changes might be odd. The rule says omit fields not present. I'll include the revision history with just revision \"3\".\n\n- Scheme: A (\"OIML-CS CERTIFICATE ISSUED UNDER SCHEME A\")\n- Member state: Switzerland\n- page_total: \"1/4\", \"2/4\", \"3/4\" → 4 pages. This fragment covers pages 1-3, but page_total is 4.\n- date_issued: not present in this fragment. Omit.\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present. Omit.\n- project_number: not present. Omit.\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- name: \"Federal Institute of Metrology METAS\" (also \"Conformity Evaluation Body METAS-Cert\")\n- address_lines: [\"Lindenweg 50, 3003 Bern-Wabern, Switzerland\"] — or split? Could be one line. I'll keep as one line or split into \"Lindenweg 50\", \"3003 Bern-Wabern\", \"Switzerland\". The table shows it as single line: \"Lindenweg 50, 3003 Bern-Wabern, Switzerland\". I'll use one line.\n- person_responsible: \"Gulian Couvreur\"\n- person_title: \"Head of METAS-Cert\"\n- phone: +41 58 387 01 11 (from footer \"phone +41 58 387 01 11\")\n- website: www.metas.ch\n\n**Applicants:**\n- Sartorius Lab Instruments GmbH & Co KG, Otto-Brenner-Strasse 20, 37079 Göttingen, Germany\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- Sartorius Scientific Instruments (Beijing) Co, Limited, address: Yu An Road No. 33, Zone B, Tianzhu Airport Industrialzone, Shunyi District, 101 300 Beijing, PR China\n\n**Certified type:**\n- category: \"Weighing instrument\" or \"Balance\"? The recommendation is R 76-1 (non-automatic weighing instruments). The type is \"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\". Category could be \"Balance\" or \"Weighing instrument\". R76 covers non-automatic weighing instruments. I'll use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" or maybe \"Balance\". Hmm. The category examples in schema: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\". For R76, likely \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" or \"Weighing instrument\". I'll use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\".\n\n- type_designations: \"BC-E and BC-A Line\" — the types. Module: \"BC-EA to BC-EH, BC-AA to BC-AH\". So the models are BC-EA, BC-EB, BC-EC, BC-ED, BC-EE, BC-EF, BC-EG, BC-EH, BC-AA, BC-AB, BC-AC, BC-AD, BC-AE, BC-AF, BC-AG, BC-AH. That's 16 models.\n\ntype_designations should include \"BC-E Line\" and \"BC-A Line\"? The Type field says \"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\". I'd set type_designations: [\"BC-E Line\", \"BC-A Line\"] or [\"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\"]. Hmm. Maybe type_designations: [\"BC-E\", \"BC-A\"]? Better: [\"BC-E Line\", \"BC-A Line\"]. And model_family models: BC-EA, BC-AA, BC-EB, BC-AB, BC-EC, BC-AC, BC-ED, BC-AD, BC-EE, BC-AE, BC-EF, BC-AF, BC-EG, BC-AG, BC-EH, BC-AH.\n\n- module_designation: The \"Module\" row says \"BC-EA to BC-EH, BC-AA to BC-AH\" — but that's actually model identifiers, not an R76 module (like \"automatic weighing instrument module\"). In R76 context \"Module\" here seems to mean model designations. Actually in METAS certificates, \"Module\" field lists the model variants. So this maps to model_family. I won't set module_designation, or set it to null. Actually the schema for certified_type has module_designation for e.g. \"Analog load cell\". Here the \"Module\" label is really the model list. I'll treat it as model variants and not include module_designation. Hmm, but maybe safer to include module_designation: null? Rule 8 says omit if not present. The field IS present but its content is model designations. I'll skip module_designation and put models in model_family.\n\n- description: not really present beyond the type name. Omit or use \"Precision and analysis balances\". I'll leave description out.\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- id: \"R76\" (canonical), edition: 2006, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [\"I\", \"II\"]. The recommendation is \"OIML R 76-1, edition 2006\". So id \"R76\", maybe part 1. The schema says id canonical e.g. \"R60\". I'll use \"R76\". edition: 2006 (int). amendment: null. scheme: \"A\". accuracy_classes: [\"I\", \"II\"].\n\n**Model family:** 16 models grouped in 5 tables. The tables pair E-line and A-line models (e.g., BC-EA/BC-AA share characteristics).\n\nTable 1: columns: Type (BC-EA/BC-AA), (BC-EB/BC-AB), (BC-EB/BC-AB — note: third column repeats BC-EB/BC-AB in OCR; likely third column should be BC-EB/BC-AB with class II? Actually looking at it: column 2 is BC-EB/BC-AB class (I), column 3 is BC-EB/BC-AB class (II). Hmm — that's an OCR issue; likely the third column is a different model, maybe BC-EB 0.1g version or something. But we can't fabricate. The OCR shows \"BC-EB BC-AB\" twice. Probably the original has BC-EB/BC-AB in class I and ... hmm. Actually Sartorius BC-E series: BC-EA, BC-EB, BC-EC, BC-ED, BC-EE, BC-EF, BC-EG, BC-EH — 8 models. BC-A: BC-AA to BC-AH — 8 models. Table 1 has 3 columns for... EA/AA, EB/AB, and a third column that should be another pair. Given classes I, I, II. Perhaps the third is \"BC-EB/BC-AB\" with different Max range \"0,1 g - 100 g\". Hmm, maybe it's actually \"BC-EA/BC-AA\" in a second variant? We can't know. I'll reproduce as given: the third column header as \"BC-EB/BC-AB\" — but that would duplicate model ids with different characteristics. \n\nHmm. Let me think. In matrix tables, if two columns have the same header, rows would conflict. For model_level characteristics, models must be uniquely identified. Options:\n1. Treat the third column as a distinct variant, e.g. model_id \"BC-EB/BC-AB (II)\" or \"BC-EB/BC-AB [0.1g-100g]\".\n2. Note it in matrix_tables with duplicate keys.\n\nI think the safest is to keep matrix_tables faithful to the OCR (columns as given, possibly with a note/description), and for model_family create model entries. Since the header is duplicated, maybe the actual document distinguishes them, e.g., \"BC-EB 0,1g / BC-AB 0,1g\" vs \"BC-EB 1g / BC-AB 1g\". Since Max is \"50 g - 220 g\" vs \"0,1 g - 100 g\", these are clearly different variants.\n\nI'll create model ids: for column 3, \"BC-EB/BC-AB (0.1 g – 100 g)\"? That's fabricating a label based on data. Alternatively, keep as given and disambiguate with \"(II)\" class: \"BC-EB/BC-AB (II)\". Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe the third column header is correct in the original as BC-EB/BC-AB but this represents a second configuration. In METAS e-cert for R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04, let me recall... I don't have direct knowledge. The Sartorius Cubis BC-E... Actually \"BC-E\" might be \"Balance Cubis Extended\"? Sartorius \"Cubis\" balances have models like MSA... BC could be \"Building Block Concept\". Hmm.\n\nActually the original certificate: METAS R076/2006-A-CH1-2019.04, Sartorius precision and analysis balances. The first table: BC-EA/BC-AA, BC-EB/BC-AB, BC-EB/BC-AB — likely one is \"BC-EB\" and other \"BC-EBi\" or similar. Without more info, I'll include a footnote-like description in matrix_tables noting the duplicate.\n\nFor model_family models, I'll list: BC-EA, BC-AA, BC-EB, BC-AB (×2 variants disambiguated), BC-EC, BC-AC, BC-ED, BC-AD, BC-EE, BC-AE, BC-EF, BC-AF, BC-EG, BC-AG, BC-EH, BC-AH.\n\nApproach: In matrix_tables rows, use column keys col1..col5? The schema for matrix_tables has columns[] and rows[] object per row. I could name columns: [\"type_group\", \"max\", \"e\", \"d\", \"n\", ...] and rows keyed by model group. Let me structure:\n\nmatrix_tables: 5 tables (one per physical table):\n\nTable 1 \"Metrological characteristics – BC-EA/BC-AA, BC-EB/BC-AB\":\ncolumns: [\"type\", \"accuracy_class\", \"max\", \"e\", \"d\", \"n\", \"temperature_range_1\", \"temperature_range_2\", \"nominal_capacity_load_receptor\", \"initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load\", \"max_weighing_pan_size\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, \"g\", ...]. Hmm but max, e, d each span ranges with units g/mg. Mixing units per column is awkward: Max \"50 g - 320 g\" (g), e \"1 mg - 2 mg\" (mg), d \"0.1 mg - 2 mg\" (mg), n unitless (count). Temperature °C. So column_units per column works since each column has consistent unit type? Max column: g for all. e: mg. d: mg. n: none. Temperature: °C. Initial zero setting: g. Pan size: mm (with Ø). OK.\n\nrows:\n- {\"type\": \"BC-EA / BC-AA\", \"accuracy_class\": \"I\", \"max\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 320}, \"e\": {\"min\": 1, \"max\": 2}, ...} — but the schema example shows numeric values in rows, not structured objects. The example: {\"size\": \"G2.5\", \"qmax\": 4.0, ...} — plain numbers with column_units. For ranges, hmm. The schema example rows are flat numbers. For ranges I could store as string \"50–320\" or as object {min,max}. The rules emphasize structured values for characteristics. For matrix tables, I think it's acceptable to use {min,max} objects or strings. To be consistent with the strict structured-value philosophy but keep matrix tables simple, I'll use objects {min, max} for ranges. Actually the example shows plain numbers. But nothing prohibits objects. Given rule 2 says EVERY characteristic value must be structured, and matrix rows aren't exactly characteristics... I'll use {min,max} objects for range values to preserve information, and it remains valid JSON.\n\nAlternatively, since model_level characteristics are required anyway, maybe matrix tables are redundant. Rule 4 R137 example emits BOTH matrix_tables AND model_level. So I should do both.\n\nGiven 16 models × ~10 attributes, model_level will be big but fine.\n\nLet me define the model groups:\n\nTable 1 (page 2/4 first table):\n- Col A: BC-EA / BC-AA, Class I, Max 50–320 g, e 1–2 mg, d 0.1–2 mg, n ≤ 320000, Tare: until 100% of Max, Temp1 +17/+27 °C, Temp2¹ +10/+30 °C, Load receptor nominal capacity 384 g, Initial zero setting + dead load² ≤ 234 g, Pan size Ø 90 mm.\n- Col B: BC-EB / BC-AB, Class I, Max 50–220 g, e 1–2 mg, d 0.1–2 mg, n ≤ 220000, Temp1 +17/+27, Temp2 +10/+30, capacity 264 g, ≤ 214 g, Ø 90 mm.\n- Col C: BC-EB / BC-AB (as given), Class II, Max 0.1–100 g, e 1–2 mg, d 0.1–2 mg, n ≤ 100000, Temp1 +10/+30, Temp2 +10/+30, capacity 264 g, ≤ 263 g, Ø 90 mm.\n\nNote \"0,1 g\" → 0.1 g.\n\nTable 2:\n- Col A: BC-EC / BC-AC, Class I, Max 500–1500 g, e 10–20 mg, d 1–20 mg, n ≤ 150000, Temp1 +17/+27, Temp2 +10/+30, capacity 1800 g, ≤1300 g, Ø 120 mm.\n- Col B: BC-ED / BC-AD, Class II, Max 1–650 g, e 0.01–0.1 g, d 0.001–0.1 g, n ≤ 65000, Temp1 +10/+30, Temp2 +10/+30, capacity 780 g, ≤ 779 g, Ø 120 mm.\n\nTable 3 (page 3/4):\n- Col A: BC-EE / BC-AE, Class II, Max 500–6200 g, e 0.1–1 g, d 0.01–1 g, n ≤ 62000, Tare 100% Max, Temp1 +10/+30 (colspan 2), capacity 7440 g, initial zero 6940 g (note: no ≤ in OCR for this one: \"6 940 g\"), pan 180 mm × 180 mm.\n- Col B: BC-EF / BC-AF, Class II, Max 500–6200 g, e 0.1–1 g, d 0.01–1 g, n ≤ 62000, Temp +10/+30, capacity 7440 g, 6940 g, pan Ø 180 mm.\n\nTable 4:\n- Col A: BC-EG / BC-AG, Class II, Max 5000–12200 g, e 1 g, d 0.1–1 g, n ≤ 12200, Temp +10/+30, capacity 14640 g, ≤ 9640 g, pan 180 × 180 mm.\n- Col B: BC-EH / BC-AH, Class II, Max 5000–12200 g, e 1 g, d 0.1–1 g, n ≤ 12200, Temp +10/+30, capacity 14640 g, ≤ 9640 g, pan Ø 180 mm.\n\nFootnotes:\n1) Only for weighing instruments with incorporated span adjustment device being automatically released.\n2) The sum of Max, initial zero setting range and dead load shall not exceed the nominal load of the load receptor.\n\nAlso there's \"Temperature range 1\" vs \"Temperature range 2¹)\" and in table 4 just \"Temperature range\". And note table 3/4 don't have Temperature range 2 (no footnote 1 marker there... table 3 has no temp2 row; table 4 has \"Temperature range\" only). Also tables 3 & 4 include \"Tare-balancing range: until 100% of Max\" row. Table 1 and 2 also have it.\n\nNow, duplicate header issue in table 1: columns B and C both \"BC-EB BC-AB\". I need to handle. Options: In matrix table, name rows by column index: \"BC-EB/BC-AB (col 2)\" — ugly. Alternatively I add a description noting the third column header as printed duplicates the second and is distinguished by class II and Max 0.1–100 g.\n\nFor model_family.models, I could list: \"BC-EB/BC-AB (I)\" and \"BC-EB/BC-AB (II)\" as two variants. That's a defensible disambiguation without fabricating new model numbers. I'll use that as model_id, and also include individual letters? Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe the intended reading: BC-EA to BC-EH is 8 models, BC-AA to BC-AH is 8 models. Table 1 col A covers BC-EA & BC-AA; col B covers BC-EB & BC-AB; col C should be another pair... but all 8 E models appear: EA, EB, EC, ED, EE, EF, EG, EH across tables 1-4 (col counts: table1 3 cols, table2 2, table3 2, table4 2 → total 9 columns for 8 pairs). So indeed 9 columns; one pair gets two columns. The duplicated header \"BC-EB BC-AB\" in col C is likely correct in the original — i.e., BC-EB/BC-AB exists in two variants: class I (50–220 g) and class II (0.1–100 g). Possibly they are different sub-versions. So disambiguating with class is right: \"BC-EB/BC-AB (I)\" and \"BC-EB/BC-AB (II)\".\n\nHmm, but actually maybe the second column's real content is \"BC-EB 1g/BC-AB 1g\" etc. I'll go with class-suffix disambiguation and note in description.\n\nNow model_family.models: Should models be individual (16 entries: BC-EA, BC-AA, ...) or pairs (9 groups)? Since characteristics apply to pairs, and matrix tables are per pair, I'll use pair-based model ids: \"BC-EA/BC-AA\", \"BC-EB/BC-AB (I)\", \"BC-EB/BC-AB (II)\", \"BC-EC/BC-AC\", \"BC-ED/BC-AD\", \"BC-EE/BC-AE\", \"BC-EF/BC-AF\", \"BC-EG/BC-AG\", \"BC-EH/BC-AH\". \n\nHmm, but model_id ideally is the model designation. Pairs share everything, so pairing is fine. Alternatively list each model individually with attributes. That doubles work. I'll use pairs, and in certified_type.type_designations list \"BC-E\" and \"BC-A\" lines plus maybe enumerate. I think type_designations: [\"BC-E Line\", \"BC-A Line\"]? The Type field: \"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\". I'll do type_designations: [\"BC-E\", \"BC-A\"] with description \"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\". Hmm, or type_designations: [\"BC-EA\", \"BC-EB\", ..., all 16]. The schema says type_designations: all type/model identifiers mentioned. So I could list all 16 model ids. That seems heavy but compliant. I'll list the two family names \"BC-E Line\" and \"BC-A Line\" plus... hmm.\n\nLet me decide: type_designations: [\"BC-E Line\", \"BC-A Line\"]. And model_family.family_name: \"Precision and analysis balances BC-E and BC-A Line\". models: 9 pair entries with label including both designations. Good.\n\nNow model_level characteristics. Attributes that vary: max, e, d, n, temperature_range_1, temperature_range_2 (only tables 1-2), nominal_capacity_of_load_receptor, initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load, max_weighing_pan_size. Also accuracy_class varies (I vs II) — could be an attribute: accuracy_class. Tare-balancing range is constant (\"until 100% of Max\") → type_level.\n\nTemperature range 2 has footnote 1 marker. Initial zero setting + dead load has footnote 2 marker.\n\nFor temperature ranges: values like \"+17 °C / +27 °C\" → {min: 17, max: 27}, unit \"°C\".\n\nn: \"≤ 320 000\" → {min: null, max: 320000}, unit null (dimensionless count). Or unit \"intervals\"? Not stated; unit null.\n\nMax: \"50 g - 320 g\" → {min: 50, max: 320}, unit \"g\". Note \"1 500 g\" → 1500, \"6 200 g\" → 6200, \"12 200 g\" → 12200, \"14 640 g\" → 14640.\n\ne: \"1 mg - 2 mg\" → {min:1, max:2}, \"mg\". \"10 mg - 20 mg\" → {min:10,max:20} mg. \"0.01 g – 0.1 g\" → {min:0.01,max:0.1} g. \"0.1 g - 1 g\" → {min:0.1,max:1} g. \"1 g\" → 1 g.\n\nd: \"0.1 mg - 2 mg\" → {0.1,2} mg; \"1 mg - 20 mg\" → {1,20} mg; \"0.001 g – 0.1 g\" → {0.001,0.1} g; \"0.01 g - 1 g\" → {0.01,1} g; \"0.1 g - 1 g\" → {0.1,1} g.\n\nPan size: \"Ø 90 mm\" → value \"Ø 90 mm\"? Unit mm, value 90, and shape round (Ø). Could add \"max_weighing_pan_size\" with value 90, unit \"mm\", and maybe a note. Or value string \"Ø 90 mm\". Rules: separate unit. The Ø indicates diameter (circular pan). For \"180 mm x 180 mm\" rectangular. I could add attribute \"weighing_pan_shape\": \"round\"/\"rectangular\". Hmm, that's extra interpretation but reasonable. Simpler: value as string \"Ø 90 mm\" with unit null? That violates unit separation. Let me do: max_weighing_pan_size {value: 90, unit \"mm\"} plus separate \"weighing_pan_size_shape\": {\"Ø\"}? Too noisy. Alternative: include the dimension as string: {value: {min:90,max:90}}? No.\n\nI'll do: attribute \"maximum_weighing_pan_size\", value: \"Ø 90\" hmm. Actually simplest compliant: value: 90, unit_symbol: \"mm\", and keep the Ø info via a separate characteristic \"weighing_pan_shape\": {value: \"round (Ø 90 mm)\"}? Overkill.\n\nLet me just make the value a string for the pan size since it's a dimension description: {value: \"Ø 90 mm\", unit_symbol: null}? The