al capacity of the load receptor: 384 g, 264 g, 264 g, 1800 g, 780 g, 7440 g, 7440 g, 14640 g, 14640 g.\n\nTare-balancing range: type_level: value \"until 100% of Max\" — string, maybe attribute \"tare_balancing_range\" with value {min: 0, max: 100}, unit \"%\" and reference Max. Like the example: \"150% of Emax\" → 150 \"%\" + separate reference. So: tare_balancing_range: {value: {min: null, max: 100}, unit_symbol: \"%\"} and tare_balancing_reference: {value: \"Max\"}. Or simply value string \"until 100% of Max\". I'll do the structured version: value 100, unit %, plus reference attribute \"max\". I'll include \"tare_balancing_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0, \"max\": 100}? Actually \"until 100% of Max\" means up to 100% → {min: null, max: 100}. Hmm, \"until\" = up to. I'll use {min: null, max: 100}.\n\nActually simpler: value: 100, unit \"%\", footnote none, and add \"tare_balancing_range_reference\": \"Max\". Let me follow the rule-150% example: value 150 unit %, separate safe_overload_reference \"Emax\". Analogously tare_balancing_range: 100 \"%\", tare_balancing_reference: \"Max\". Good.\n\nTemperature range 2 only applies to tables 1 & 2 models (EA..ED, AA..AD groups). For others, temperature_range_2 absent — don't fabricate; omit values for those models. Note tables 3 & 4 have only \"Temperature range\" (or \"Temperature range 1\"). Table 3 header says \"Temperature range 1\" (colspan 2), table 4 says \"Temperature range\". I'll treat all as temperature_range_1.\n\nAccuracy class: include as model_level attribute with values \"I\"/\"II\" (string, unit null).\n\nNow the counts: model_level entries: max, e, d, n, accuracy_class, temperature_range_1, temperature_range_2, nominal_capacity_of_load_receptor, initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load, maximum_weighing_pan_size → 10 entries × 9 models each. That's fine.\n\nModel ids: I'll use:\n- \"BC-EA/BC-AA\"\n- \"BC-EB/BC-AB (accuracy class I)\" hmm long. Maybe \"BC-EB/BC-AB (I)\" and \"BC-EB/BC-AB (II)\".\n- \"BC-EC/BC-AC\"\n- \"BC-ED/BC-AD\"\n- \"BC-EE/BC-AE\"\n- \"BC-EF/BC-AF\"\n- \"BC-EG/BC-AG\"\n- \"BC-EH/BC-AH\"\n\n9 model groups. And in models[], label can describe: e.g. {\"model_id\": \"BC-EA/BC-AA\", \"label\": \"BC-EA (class I) / BC-AA (class I)\"}? Keep simpler: label omitted or \"Class I\". I'll add attributes like accuracy_class? The models[] entries have attributes{} — optional. I could put accuracy_class in attributes per model. But then also model_level accuracy_class duplicates. Rule: model_level for attribute×model matrix. I'll include accuracy_class in model_level only.\n\nAlso should models[] list the individual 16 names? I'll set model_id as the pair string; that's compact.\n\nmatrix_tables: I'll produce 4 tables (physical tables on pages 2/4 and 3/4). Wait, page 2/4 has 2 tables, page 3/4 has 2 tables → 4 matrix tables total. Names: \"Metrological characteristics – BC-EA/AA, BC-EB/AB, BC-EB/AB (II)\"; \"Metrological characteristics – BC-EC/AC, BC-ED/AD\"; \"Metrological characteristics – BC-EE/AE, BC-EF/AF\"; \"Metrological characteristics – BC-EG/AG, BC-EH/AH\".\n\ncolumns: [\"type\", \"accuracy_class\", \"max\", \"e\", \"d\", \"n\", \"tare_balancing_range\", \"temperature_range_1\", \"temperature_range_2\", \"nominal_capacity_of_load_receptor\", \"initial_zero_setting_plus_dead_load\", \"max_weighing_pan_size\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, \"g\", ...]. Hmm but max, e, d each span ranges with units g/mg. Mixing units per column is awkward: Max \"50 g - 320 g\" (g), e \"1 mg - 2 mg\" (mg), d \"0.1 mg - 2 mg\" (mg), n unitless (count). Temperature °C. So column_units per column works since each column has consistent unit type? Max column: g for all. e: mg. d: mg. n: none. Temperature: °C. Initial zero setting: g. Pan size: mm (with Ø). OK.\n\nrows values: use structured {min,max} objects for ranges, numbers for scalars, strings for pan size and tare. E.g. row: {\"type\": \"BC-EA / BC-AA\", \"accuracy_class\": \"I\", \"max\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 320}, \"e\